====Preposition====
{{en-head|prep}}

# From one [[side]] or [[end]] of (something) to the [[other]]. 
## So as to [[enter]] (something), pass [[within]] or [[across]], and then [[leave]].
##: {{ux|en|I drove '''through''' Lausanne on my way from Geneva to Zurich.}}
##: {{ux|en|The bullet went right '''through''' his leg.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838
|page=13 (Technology Quarterly)
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21578516-transport-new-train-technologies-are-less-visible-and-spread-less-quickly|title=Ideas coming down the track
|passage=A “moving platform” scheme{{...}}is more technologically ambitious than maglev trains even though it relies on conventional rails. Local trains would use side-by-side rails to roll alongside intercity trains and allow passengers to switch trains by stepping '''through''' docking bays.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130810234419/http://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21578516-transport-new-train-technologies-are-less-visible-and-spread-less-quickly}}
## So as to progress within (something) or towards the end or limit of (something).
##: {{ux|en|The virus broke out in the capital city and is now spreading quickly '''through''' the country.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm working '''through''' this [[box set]] of the TV series 'Downton Abbey'.}}
##: {{ux|en|They forced a path '''through''' the jungle.}}
## From one side of (an opening) to the other.
##: {{ux|en|Go straight ahead and '''through''' that door.}}
##: {{ux|en|Look '''through''' the window.}}
## To or beyond the other side of (an obstacle); [[past]].
##: {{ux|en|I hope to start in the autumn, but I've got to get '''through''' the interview first.}} 
##: {{ux|en|The finance bill did not get '''through''' Congress.}} 
##: {{ux|en|We're '''through''' the worst part.}} 
##: {{ux|en|The going will be easier once we're '''through''' the bog.}} 
##: {{ux|en|You can't see '''through''' a brick wall!}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=76
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21579801-making-sense-statistics-riddle-our-days-snakes-and-ladders|title=Snakes and ladders
|passage=Risk is everywhere.{{...}}For each one there is a frighteningly precise measurement of just how likely it is to jump from the shadows and get you. “The Norm Chronicles”{{...}}aims to help data-phobes find their way '''through''' this blizzard of risks.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130813173359/http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21579801-making-sense-statistics-riddle-our-days-snakes-and-ladders}}
## {{lb|en|in phrases such as 'go through', 'get through' etc.}} {{n-g|Indicating that something has been [[consume]]d or [[use up|used up]].}}
##: {{ux|en|Have you got '''through''' all those chocolates already?!}}
##: {{ux|en|The company went '''through''' its £100m startup investment in six months.}}
## Along the course of; {{n-g|used in expressions of progress towards the end of something}}.
##: {{ux|en|We're halfway '''through''' the year.}} 
## Throughout the duration of.
##: {{ux|en|He worked '''through''' the night.}}
##: {{ux|en|I never want to sit '''through''' another meeting like that again.}}
# {{cap|via}} or by way of.
## By way of (a physical passage).
##: {{ux|en|The hot water enters '''through''' this pipe.}}
## By way of (an intermediary, agent, medium, etc.).
##: {{ux|en|I met her '''through''' a friend of mine.}}
##: {{ux|en|This matter was dealt with '''through''' our London office.}}
##: {{ux|en|I received this anonymous letter '''through''' the post.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-05-25|volume=407|issue=8837|page=74
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21578357-plan-assess-peoples-personal-characteristics-their-twitter-streams-no|title=No hiding place
|passage=Inf America alone, people spent $170 billion on “direct marketing”—junk mail of both the physical and electronic varieties—last year. Yet of those who received unsolicited adverts '''through''' the post, only 3% bought anything as a result. If the bumf arrived electronically, the take-up rate was 0.1%. And for online adverts the “conversion” into sales was a minuscule 0.01%.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130712060459/http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21578357-plan-assess-peoples-personal-characteristics-their-twitter-streams-no}}
# Throughout or across the extent of.
#: {{ux|en|There are errors all '''through''' this report.}}
#: {{ux|en|Work the eggs and sugar '''through''' the mixture.}}
# {{cap|amidst}} or [[surrounded]] by (while moving).
#: {{ux|en|We slogged '''through''' the mud for hours before turning back and giving up.}}
#: {{ux|en|The arrow flew '''through''' the air.}}
#: {{ux|en|I spent all day wading '''through''' paperwork.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|page=16|passage=Athelstan Arundel walked home all the way, foaming and raging. {{...}} He walked the whole way, walking '''through''' crowds, and under the noses of dray-horses, carriage-horses, and cart-horses, without taking the least notice of them.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=I stumbled along '''through''' the young pines and huckleberry bushes. Pretty soon I struck into a sort of path that, I cal'lated, might lead to the road I was hunting for. It twisted and turned, and, the first thing I knew, made a sudden bend around a bunch of bayberry scrub and opened out into a big clear space like a lawn.}}
#* {{RQ:Vance Outsider|page=382|column=1|passage=Turning back, then, toward the basement staircase, she began to grope her way '''through''' blinding darkness, but had taken only a few uncertain steps when, of a sudden, she stopped short and for a little stood like a stricken thing, quite motionless save that she quaked to her very marrow in the grasp of a great and enervating fear.}}
# {{lb|en|North America}} [[to|To]] (or [[up to]]) and including, with all intermediate values; to... inclusive; until the end of.
#: {{ux|en|from 1945 '''through''' 1991;&emsp; the numbers 1 '''through''' 9;&emsp; {{nowrap|your membership is active '''through''' March 15, 2013}}}}
#: {{ux|en|The plural is "oyesses", but "oyes" vied with "oyez" '''through''' about the first half of the nineteenth century.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=UN Study: China, US, Japan Lead World AI Development|url=https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/un-study-china-us-japan-lead-world-ai-development/4769094.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190207184548/https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/un-study-china-us-japan-lead-world-ai-development/4769094.html|archivedate=7 February 2019|work=w:Voice of America|date=3 February 2019|passage=It includes patent requests in machine learning '''through''' 2016, the last year for which details are available.}}
# [[by means of|By means of]].
#: {{ux|en|This team believes in winning '''through''' intimidation.}}
#: {{ux|en|He's got to where he is today '''through''' sheer hard work and determination.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=September 28, 2011|author=Tom Rostance|title=Arsenal 2-1 Olympiakos
|work=w:BBC Sport|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14998317.stm
|passage=But the home side were ahead in the eighth minute '''through''' 18-year-old Oxlade-Chamberlain.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190721195203/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/14998317}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they|title=The attack of the MOOCs
|passage=Since the launch early last year of&nbsp;[&hellip;] two Silicon Valley start-ups offering free education '''through''' MOOCs, massive open online courses, the ivory towers of academia have been shaken to their foundations. University brands built in some cases over centuries have been forced to contemplate the possibility that information technology will rapidly make their existing business model obsolete.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130806092942/http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they}}
# In consequence of; as a result of.
#: {{ux|en|The project failed '''through''' lack of investment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Dimitri Yanuli|title=You Might Be Right, but You Ain't Right with the Word of God
|passage=Our minds and hearts are corrupted with the Adamic virus at birth, and '''through''' a lifetime of sin and tragedy, our hearts and thoughts get more evil and more corrupted as we experience life's tragedies.}}

